            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>The purpose of an ad account balance alert is not merely to notify the media buyer. It is to ensure that funding remains available throughout the expected billing cycle. Insufficient funds, delayed top-ups, declined cards, and delayed budget synchronization can all interrupt campaigns that are actively scaling. An effective system must cover alerts, funding, reconciliation, and incident response.</p>
<h2>1. How Much Balance Should Trigger an Alert?\n</h2>
<p><strong>Question: Is an alert at 20% of the remaining balance sufficient for every ad account?</strong></p>
<p>No. A universal percentage ignores differences in daily spend, top-up processing time, and payment methods. A better approach is to estimate how much the account will spend before the next top-up becomes available, then create tiered alerts based on that estimate.</p>
<p>For example, a utility app with stable daily consumption can divide its account balance into normal, watch, and critical levels. The watch level prompts the media buyer to confirm the funding plan, while the critical level requires immediate follow-up by the account owner. These percentages should not be copied blindly. Each threshold should cover the billing cycle, settlement delays, and short-term spending fluctuations.</p>
<p><strong>The key test is simple: if payment will be processed tomorrow, an alert received today is already too late.</strong> For high-spend accounts, hourly monitoring may be necessary so that a large payment does not consume the available balance before the end of the day.</p>
<h2>2. Should Balance, Budget, and Top-Up Status Be Monitored Separately?\n</h2>
<p><strong>Question: The campaign still has budget. Why did delivery stop?</strong></p>
<p>Account balance, campaign budget, and payment status are separate systems. A campaign budget does not prove that the account has usable funds, and a top-up marked as processing does not mean the money is available yet. Media buyers who only monitor the campaign budget can therefore misdiagnose the issue.</p>
<p>Balance alerts should connect at least three data points: current available balance, the timestamp of the last successful debit, and the next expected debit amount. If the balance is falling significantly faster than usual, the team should also check for abnormal spending, duplicate charges, or sudden budget increases.</p>
<p><strong>A reliable setup separates financial monitoring from platform notifications.</strong> Platform alerts should still be enabled, but the team also needs a shared funding ledger recording each account, currency, payment method, expected settlement date, and usable balance. This prevents critical information from being scattered across individual backends.</p>
<h2>3. How Should Payment Methods and Top-Up Timing Be Managed?\n</h2>
<p><strong>Question: We topped up the account, but campaigns still stopped. What happened?</strong></p>
<p>Common causes include insufficient card limits, failed payment retries, cross-border payment reviews, holiday processing delays, and authorization holds. A successful top-up does not necessarily mean that funds are immediately available. The account’s usable balance and actual settlement status should be treated as the source of truth.</p>
<p>For each standard payment card, record its typical transaction limit, previous failure reasons, and processing time. Maintain compliant backup payment methods as well. Backup cards should support the same business account operationally, not serve as a way to create duplicate accounts or bypass platform reviews.</p>
<p>High-spend accounts should have an action window before the expected debit date instead of waiting for a low-balance notice. Whether a top-up requires manual confirmation should depend on its actual processing cycle, not on a fixed waiting period.</p>
<h2>4. How Can Teams Monitor Multiple Ad Accounts Without Missing One?\n</h2>
<p><strong>Question: With more than a dozen accounts managed by different people, how do we prevent an overlooked low balance?</strong></p>
<p>Checking every account manually makes the alert process unreliable. A centralized overview or shared ledger should show each account’s balance, consumption rate, last debit time, funding status, and responsible owner.</p>
<p>Access should follow clear roles. Media buyers manage campaigns, finance manages payments and reconciliation, and administrators oversee the full account portfolio. Avoid sharing one administrator login, and do not rely on verbal updates from individual buyers.</p>
<p><strong>The operational test is whether the owner can identify every account that may run short within the next 24 to 48 hours from a single screen.</strong> If that is not possible, the account structure, balance definitions, or ownership assignments need to be reorganized.</p>
<h2>5. What Should Happen When a Potential Interruption Is Detected?\n</h2>
<p><strong>Question: After an urgent alert, which actions should come first?</strong></p>
<p>First confirm whether the issue is related to funding or campaign delivery. This prevents the team from taking action in the wrong area.</p>
<ol>
<li>Check the available balance and any failed-payment notices.</li>
<li>Review recent spending and budget changes to rule out abnormal scale-up.</li>
<li>Inspect the payment method, failure reason, and top-up processing status.</li>
<li>Fund accounts confirmed to require additional funds and record the expected settlement time.</li>
<li>If the balance cannot be restored immediately, adjust the budgets or pacing of non-priority campaigns.</li>
<li>After funding is restored, verify the debit amount and update the balance ledger.</li>
</ol>
<p>Do not automatically pause every campaign when an alert arrives, and do not increase budgets without analysis. Whether priority campaigns should continue depends on spend, conversion performance, and creative results. Balance alerts protect funding continuity; they do not replace campaign performance management.</p>
<p>The final SOP is straightforward: estimate the consumption cycle, set risk-based alerts, confirm top-up settlement, centralize multi-account monitoring, diagnose before controlling budgets, and reconcile every debit. This turns balance management from emergency troubleshooting into a stable safeguard for continuous advertising.</p>
